60,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option C is too risky due to the lottery and rigid application process. Option B's separate accommodation for commuters makes costs unpredictable. Option A offers a dedicated building and meals, which provides the most financial stability.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Toshio,,Option B is out; the hall's schedule is too crowded and self-catering is a burden we can't repeat. Option A is the most viable as it removes the cooking duty and avoids the administrative nightmare of Option C.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Minako,,"Option B is unacceptable as it separates members by residence, worsening the social divide. Option C's shared dorms are ideal for cohesion, but Option A's dedicated building also ensures everyone stays together. I support Option A.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Option C's shared dorms sound like the crowded conditions I read about, which would ruin everyone's sleep. Option B is too fragmented. Option A seems the best for ensuring members can actually rest and recover.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji @Toshio, while Option A seems convenient, a dedicated building often implies a whole-building let with a non-refundable deposit. If members cancel, the cost per person will spike, creating the exact financial unfairness I want to avoid.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, that is a risk, but Option C's lottery is a gamble we cannot afford. Option A's meals and dedicated space allow for a more predictable total cost than Option B's fragmented lodging. We can manage the deposit risk.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Asuka, the risk of a deposit is preferable to the certainty of burnout. Option A eliminates the cooking duties and the scheduling conflicts of Option B. We must protect the organizers' time to ensure the camp's success.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, the risk of a price spike is manageable, but the risk of club collapse is not. Option A keeps everyone under one roof, which is the only way to stop the attrition we've seen.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Asuka, I understand your concern about the cost, but we must prioritize the members' well-being. Option A provides a dedicated space and meals, which is far more conducive to recovery than the crowded dorms of Option C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji @Toshio, managing the deposit risk is not a simple matter; it's a matter of who pays when someone drops out. Option C's per-head pricing is the only way to ensure absolute financial fairness for every member.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, absolute fairness is a myth if we fail to secure a booking due to the lottery. Option C is a gamble. Option A's fixed costs are a known quantity we can budget for accurately.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Asuka, we cannot prioritize a theoretical pricing model over the actual labor required. Option C's administrative burden and Option B's cooking duties are guaranteed stressors. Option A is the only choice that protects the organizers' sanity.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, we cannot let the fear of a price spike paralyze us. If we choose Option C and fail the lottery, or Option B and further isolate members, we lose more than just money. Option A is the best compromise.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Asuka, while fairness is important, the physical toll of poor sleep in Option C would be a far greater cost to the members. Option A provides the stability and comfort necessary for a successful camp.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji @Toshio, you call it a theoretical risk, but it is a practical certainty that late cancellations happen. Option A's structure penalizes those who actually attend. Option C is the only one that guarantees no one pays for another's absence.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, we cannot risk the lottery of Option C or the fragmentation of Option B. Option A is the only choice that provides the stability and control we need to avoid a deficit. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"@Asuka, the risk of a deposit is a financial calculation, but the burden of cooking and scheduling is a physical and mental toll. We cannot repeat last year's mistakes. For the sake of the organizers, I vote for Option A.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, financial fairness is important, but the survival of the club depends on our members feeling connected. Option A ensures we stay together and bond. For the future of our community, I vote for Option A.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,"@Asuka, I respect your commitment to fairness, but we cannot ignore the physical needs of the members. Option A is the only choice that guarantees both cohesion and proper rest. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"Despite my strong objections regarding the financial risk of a whole-building let, it is clear that the majority prioritizes administrative ease and social cohesion. I cannot justify a choice that penalizes attendees, but I will yield. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
